Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
for_each_possible_cpu(cpu)
    intel_pstate_update_max_freq(all_cpu_data[cpu]);

Reason: The loop iterates over all possible CPUs, but offline CPUs have `all_cpu_data[cpu]` set to NULL. Passing this NULL pointer to `intel_pstate_update_max_freq` (which takes a `struct cpudata*` and immediately dereferences `cpudata->cpu`) causes a NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
static bool intel_pstate_update_max_freq(int cpu) {
    struct cpufreq_policy *policy __free(put_cpufreq_policy) = cpufreq_cpu_get(cpu);
    if (!policy)
        return false;
    __intel_pstate_update_max_freq(policy, all_cpu_data[cpu]);
    return true;
}
...
for_each_possible_cpu(cpu)
    intel_pstate_update_max_freq(cpu);

Reason: The function now accepts a CPU number instead of a `cpudata` pointer. It only uses `all_cpu_data[cpu]` after verifying that a valid `cpufreq_policy` exists for that CPU (which ensures the CPU is online). The loop passes the CPU number, so offline CPUs are safely skipped when `cpufreq_cpu_get` returns NULL, preventing the NULL pointer dereference.

